Accessory structures shall include all detached (meaning freestanding and independent) structures, regardless of use. Structures attached to the principal building in a substantial manner by an adjoining roof, wall or foundation are considered additions and must meet requirements applicable to the principal building. Accessory structures shall be permitted provided that they adhere to the requirements of this Ordinance. All attached or detached Accessory Buildings in residential zoning districts shall meet the following requirements:
(a) All Accessory Buildings shall have the same side yard setback required for the principal structure as specified elsewhere in this Ordinance;
(b) No Accessory Building shall be constructed on a lot prior to the time of the construction of the principal building to which it is an accessory;
(c) All Accessory Buildings shall have a rear yard setback of not less than ten feet, including eaves and other overhanging portions of the structure;
(d) No Accessory Building shall be closer than twenty feet (20 ft.) to any other structure on the same lot or parcel of land;
(e) All Accessory Buildings shall be constructed on either concrete poured slabs, pole design or concrete block;
(f) The aggregate square footage of all Accessory Buildings on a lot shall not exceed 50 percent of the heated ground floor area of the principal structure;
(g) No Accessory Building shall be permitted in the front yard. Accessory Buildings are permitted only in rear or side yards;
(h) No Accessory Building shall exceed fifteen feet (15 ft.) in height.
9.13.1 Accessory Buildings, Portable.
(a) Portable Accessory Buildings are prohibited in commercial and industrial zoning districts.
(b) Portable Accessory Buildings shall be permitted in residential zoning districts provided the following conditions are met:
(1) The dimensions of a portable accessory building shall not exceed 250 square feet;
(2) Portable Accessory Buildings shall not be placed in the front yard of a residence;
(3) A residential lot shall have no more than one Portable Accessory Building;
(4) Portable Accessory Buildings shall not be used as a residence or dwelling;
(5) All setback requirements relating to Accessory Buildings must be met for rear and side yards;
(6) The owner shall obtain a building permit from the City prior to constructing, moving, placing or erecting a Portable Accessory Building on a lot.
(c) Portable Accessory Buildings shall be allowed on a lot prior to construction of the principal structure only when a valid building permit has been issued for construction of the principal structure. In the event the building permit for the principal structure expires or is revoked, the Portable Accessory Building must be removed from the property. This period shall commence on the date of issuance of the building permit for the principal structure and end at the time the building permit expires or is revoked.

