12.4.1 Zoning District Regulations. Every building development erected and maintained under the provisions of this Article shall comply with all of the provisions established by this Ordinance for the zoning district in which the building group is located except the regulation requiring a separate building lot to be provided and maintained for each principal structure.
12.4.2 Site Plan and Improvements. A special plan for a PUD shall address, and there shall be provided, the following:
(a) Drainage: Adequate facilities for the drainage of surface water, including storm sewers, gutters, paving and the proper design of finished grades, when requested by the Zoning Administrator;
(b) Ingress and Egress: Adequate facilities for the convenient circulation of pedestrian and vehicular traffic, including sidewalks, driveways, off-street parking areas, off-street loading areas, and landscaped separation spaces between pedestrian and vehicular ways;
(c) Location of all buildings, structures or dwellings;
(d) Recreation Area: adequate and appropriately located play areas for children;
(e) Protection of Residential District: In commercial building developments near or adjoining residential districts, adequate provision (including fences, walls and/or planting) to screen and protect the residential districts from parking lot illumination, headlights, fumes, heat, noise, blowing papers, dust, and the visual encroachment of commercial activity on the privacy and neighborhood character of the residential area;
(f) A landscaping plan of the entire project showing individual plants and vegetation types;
(g) Location of all garbage, trash and/or dumpster receptacles and methods to properly screen from view;
(h) Formation of an Incorporated Non-Profit Association (when applicable and/or when required by the City Council as part of a PUD). When the formation of an Incorporated Non-Profit Association is required, as part of a PUD, the developer shall:
(1) Create an incorporated non-profit homeowners association, which assures exterior maintenance of each unit and the common areas;
(2) Record covenants in the office of the Superior Court Clerk of Jefferson County which automatically make every lot owner an association member; provide the lot owners the right to use the common property; and establish his/her voting rights in the association and his/her obligation to pay its assessments.

